Below is a small, self‑contained example that shows

1. A class (`MyResource`) that **implements `IDisposable`**.  
2. How the **`using` statement** guarantees that `Dispose` is called even if an exception occurs.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;

#region 1. A disposable type
/// <summary>
/// A simple wrapper around a FileStream that writes text.
/// Demonstrates the full Dispose pattern.
/// </summary>
public sealed class MyResource : IDisposable
{
    // The managed resource
    private readonly FileStream _fileStream;

    // To detect redundant calls
    private bool _disposed = false;

    public MyResource(string filePath)
    {
        // Open (or create) the file.  The FileStream itself is disposable.
        _fileStream = new FileStream(filePath, FileMode.OpenOrCreate, FileAccess.Write);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Writes a string to the file.
    /// Throws ObjectDisposedException if the object has already been disposed.
    /// </summary>
    public void Write(string text)
    {
        if (_disposed)
            throw new ObjectDisposedException(nameof(MyResource));

        byte[] bytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(text);
        _fileStream.Write(bytes, 0, bytes.Length);
        _fileStream.Flush();        // Ensure data is written to disk
    }

    #region Dispose pattern
    // Protected implementation of Dispose.
    // 'disposing' is true when called from Dispose(), false when called from finalizer.
    private void Dispose(bool disposing)
    {
        if (_disposed)
            return;

        if (disposing)
        {
            // Dispose managed resources
            _fileStream?.Dispose();
        }

        // Here you would free any unmanaged resources, if you had any.

        _disposed = true;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Public implementation of IDisposable.
    /// </summary>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        Dispose(disposing: true);
        // Suppress finalization because the cleanup is already done.
        GC.SuppressFinalize(this);
    }

    // Finalizer – called if the consumer forgets to dispose.
    ~MyResource()
    {
        Dispose(disposing: false);
    }
    #endregion
}
#endregion

#region 2. Consuming the disposable type
class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // Option 1 – traditional using statement (pre‑C# 8)
        using (var resource = new MyResource("example.txt"))
        {
            resource.Write("Hello, World!\n");
            resource.Write("This file will be closed automatically.\n");
        }   // Dispose() is called automatically here, even if an exception occurs.

        // Option 2 – C# 8+ using declaration (more concise)
        // using var resource2 = new MyResource("example2.txt");
        // resource2.Write("Hello from using declaration!");

        // Trying to use 'resource' after the using block will throw
        // (the variable is out of scope, so you can’t actually access it here).
        Console.WriteLine("File has been written and the resource has been disposed.");
    }
}
#endregion
```

### What this example demonstrates

| Feature | Code |
|---------|------|
| **`IDisposable` implementation** | `public sealed class MyResource : IDisposable` |
| **Dispose pattern (managed + optional unmanaged)** | `Dispose(bool disposing)` + finalizer |
| **Using statement** | `using (var resource = new MyResource(...)) { ... }` |
| **Automatic disposal even on exceptions** | The `using` block ensures `Dispose` is called regardless of how the block exits. |
| **Suppressing finalization** | `GC.SuppressFinalize(this)` after successful disposal. |

You can run this code in any C# console application (any .NET Core/5+/6+ version will work).  
It creates two text files (`example.txt` and `example2.txt` if you uncomment the second block) and guarantees that the underlying `FileStream` is closed properly.
